-- Unit test for the `peak_rss_bytes` builtin: portable (bytes on both macOS
-- and Linux -- see runtime/march_runtime.c's normalisation of getrusage's
-- platform-dependent ru_maxrss unit), ambient (no capability grant needed --
-- see typecheck.ml, where it is deliberately absent from the capability
-- table next to the gated `unix_time`).
--
-- Only ORDER-STABLE facts are printed, never raw byte counts: RSS varies by
-- run, allocator, and platform. The two bounds on `after` are the actual
-- unit assertion -- a Linux kilobytes-vs-bytes mistake would make the value
-- ~1024x too small and fail the lower bound; mistakenly treating macOS bytes
-- as kilobytes would make it ~1024x too large and fail the upper bound.
--
-- THE BOUNDS ARE ON THE ABSOLUTE VALUE, NOT THE DELTA, and that is
-- deliberate. This test first asserted `after - before > 32000000` and went
-- RED on CI's ubuntu leg while staying green on macOS. That was NOT a unit
-- bug: 128,143,360 == 125,140 * 1024, so the Linux `* 1024` normalisation in
-- march_peak_rss_bytes had fired correctly and the process really did have a
-- ~122 MB resident peak before this fixture allocated anything. peak RSS is a
-- HIGH-WATER MARK, so when the baseline peak already exceeds what the test
-- allocates, the delta saturates at 0 no matter how correct the units are.
--
-- The two platforms' baselines are what diverge, measured on this fixture:
--   Darwin arm64   before =   2,211,840 B (2.1 MB)  -> delta ~64 MB, passed
--   ubuntu (CI)    before ~ 128,143,360 B (122 MB)  -> delta ~0,     failed
-- A delta lower bound is therefore only meaningful on a platform whose
-- baseline happens to be small -- i.e. it is not portable. The absolute
-- value is immune to baseline saturation (a bigger baseline only pushes
-- `after` further ABOVE the lower bound) while keeping full 1024x
-- falsifiability in both directions: on Darwin `after` is 66,322,432 B, so a
-- KB-as-bytes read would report ~64,768 (500x below the lower bound) and a
-- bytes-scaled-by-1024 read ~6.8e10 (8.5x above the upper bound).
-- Do not "restore" the delta form.
mod PeakRss do
  needs IO.Console
  fn main(_cap_console : Cap(IO.Console)) do
    let before = peak_rss_bytes()
    let a = NativeArray.make_u8(64000000, 7)
    let after = peak_rss_bytes()
    println(before > 0)                          -- true
    println(after >= before)                      -- true
    -- 64MB allocated, so peak RSS is at least tens of MB: NOT ~65,000 (a KB
    -- value misreported as bytes) nor ~7e10 (bytes wrongly scaled by 1024).
    -- Real peaks here are ~70 MB (macOS) to ~190 MB (Linux, whose baseline
    -- alone is ~122 MB); 8 GB leaves ~40x headroom over the largest of those
    -- while sitting ~9x BELOW the smallest value a 1024x error could produce.
    println(after > 32000000)                     -- true
    println(after < 8000000000)                   -- true
    println(NativeArray.get_u8(a, 0))              -- 7 (keeps `a` live past the measurement)
  end
end
